<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Addu Atoll</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Addu City</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Addu Atoll is the southernmost atoll of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, just south of the equator, and home to the country's second city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> built an air base on its island of Gan during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and kept it as RAF Gan until 1976. Resentment of the government in Malé over taxes and trade led its people, with neighbouring Huvadhu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuvahmulah">Fuvahmulah</a>, to form the breakaway United Suvadive Republic in 1959–1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/addu-atoll.jpg" alt="Addu Atoll" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Addu_Atoll_(Landsat).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Seenu Atoll, historically known as Addu Atoll, is the southernmost atoll of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a> and lies 540 kilometres south of Malé. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuvahmulah">Fuvahmulah</a>, 40 kilometres to the north, it extends the Maldives into the Southern Hemisphere. Unlike other atolls, its lagoon is a natural anchorage, reached by four channels: the Kuda and Maa channels on the north, the Gan channel on the south and the broad Villingili channel on the southeast.</p><p>Barrier reefs protect the islands from storms and high waves, coconut palms grow almost everywhere, and small lakes, wetlands and marshy taro fields are found nowhere else in the country. Addu City, one of the four cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, covers the inhabited islands of Hulhudhoo, Meedhoo, Maradhoo, Feydhoo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hithadhoo">Hithadhoo</a>; Gan, with its airport, lies outside it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Addu is believed to come from the local words ah, eight, and dhoo, island, because eight islands were traditionally inhabited. British mapmakers anglicised it as Addou. Some of the earliest known settlements in the country are on Meedhoo, in the far north of the atoll. Long ago families appointed by the sultans in Malé ruled Addu, and it may have had a matrilineal system of inheritance.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> built a secret base there, HMS Haitan, to use the deep anchorage and sheltered harbour. Oil tanks and storage facilities, an airstrip and a flying boat base were built on Gan, and the base stayed unknown to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> in the early years of the war. In 1957 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> as RAF Gan and closed in 1976. The island is now the site of Gan International Airport. When Addu declared independence as the United Suvadive Republic, the Malé dialect rather than the Addu dialect was used in its official correspondence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Addu people speak the Addu dialect, which resembles the Mulaku dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuvahmulah">Fuvahmulah</a> but differs somewhat from official Dhivehi; it was traditionally a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> among the educated of three southern atolls, while the written language is common throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>. The white tern is found in the Maldives only on Addu Atoll and is seen as a symbol of the islands. Addu was also the only part of the Maldives untouched by the coral bleaching of 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seenu Atoll” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Discontent in the three southernmost atolls, <b>Addu</b>, Huvadhu and Fuvahmulah, over taxes and Male's attempts to restrict trade led to revolt in 1959.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-suvadive-republic">United Suvadive Republic</a></li></ul></section>
